"There is no Jew nor Gentile, no male nor female in the eyes of God". This is a grounding moral of the Catholic Church to promote the values of Diversity, Inclusion, and Equity to its people.

Agreeing on the definitions of the key words Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion (collectively referred to as "DEI" from hereinafter) is crucial in order to maintain a succinct historical view. One could give rigid and strong book definitions of these words, but this essay aims to explore what each values to the Church meant through case studies and examples - because everyone, especially the current political and societal nuances of "DEI", interprets terminologies differently. The definitions relative to this essay is the one explored by the Church throughout history. To evaluate the varying degrees of success of different changes introduced to the Catholic Church, I will refer to John Henry Newman's Development of Doctrine, which outlines that core doctrinal teachings are not built on static monolith, but instead they grow over time organically - we will come back to this.

Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion exists as a trinity of core motivic values all interdependent on one another - just like space, matter, and time, and the Holy Trinity of the Father, Son, and Holy Spirit. One may not exist without the other. These values are grounded in the Catholic belief of "Imago Dei" (Genesis 1:27) - the belief that all human beings are created in the image of God. This view gives innate dignity to humans who are all deserving of the values of DEI. Therefore, there is a special, spiritual significance of DEI to Christians, Catholics, and all people of the world. But how were the values of DEI are executed, and what reformations to doctrine and tradition were made to facilitate this change?

A key dispute in the Catholic Church regarding DEI is the dichotomy between uniformity and plurality. It lies on a fine tension between uniformity - a unified structure and the equivalence of praise and worship of God, and plurality - a diverse expression of the faith interleaved with a whole assortment of cultural norms and practices. The inculturation (Novus Ordo) of the Catholic faith with the local culture is a large portion of the development of the core teachings of the Church to honour the values of DEI. For example, in the 17th century, European Catholic missionaries (the Jesuits, then the Franciscans and the Dominicans) travelled to China. In China, their cultural practice includes the acts of Confucianism and veneration of their ancestors. The integration of these cultural rituals to the Catholic mass by the Chinese Catholics was met with great disgust and detestation from the Dominicans and Franciscans, who were advocates of the Latin Rite. Rome's propaganda fide condemned the plurality of the liturgical structure of worship which involved said cultural practices in the early 18th century. However, in 1939, the Holy See revisited this issue and now permits ancestor veneration and Confucianism alongside other cultural rituals to be integrated in plurality to the Catholic liturgies.

This is a strong example of a case outlined by John Henry Newman, through his "Development of Doctrine" which states that religion's doctrine grows organically. Its core (acorn) stays the same, but its outward [removed]oak tree) will shift and grow to an objective moral standard over time (it converges, like an infinite sum approaching a value). We can see that the outward expression of faith - mass order, liturgical rites and inculturation of local practices and rituals - has indeed shifted from the more formal Latin Rite, to a now more welcoming Novus Ordo - a World Church - where plurality exists in the church and its people. Karl Rahner advocates for Novus Ordo, arguing that Catholicism and Christianity as a whole must move on from a "European export" to a global religious identity, to be inculturated and integrated by many different cultures and norms around the world, and outlines that inculturation is not necessarily cultural relativism. Therefore, uniformity does not depend on unity - in fact, unity is achieved when the plurality of the cultures shares a common goal - Catholicism.

Further shifts towards Novus Ordo took place with the second Vatican Council in 1962, which enabled use of vernacular languages in worship and removed other formal "european exports" - such as the use of the Latin language in masses and allowing for more diverse expressions of faith to become truly a "catholic/universal" church. With this, the Catholic Church explores "diversity" - one defined by these events to be meaning "unity in plurality".

The shift from the Latin Rite also benefited school and the school mass. With more laity participation in liturgies and masses, students gained more ways to strengthen their relationship with Christ by openly presenting themselves in front of the catholic audience through readings, psalms and eucharistic ministry - which one from the days of the Latin Rite could never dream of due to its limited lay participation. The exemplification of the DEI values in transitioning away from a strict liturgical environment to a more welcoming and familiar space paved a way for an easier future inculturation of Catholicism and its integration with cultures and practices.

However, this diversity offered by plurality means little without proper access and power from its laypeople. In other words, diversity fails to shine with the lack of equity and inclusion - a clear interdependence on one another.

This leads us to another key dispute in the changes to the Catholic Church regarding DEI is the debate between ontological equality and functional hierarchy, especially on the gender division in the hierarchy and power structures of the Catholic Church. In 1994, the Vatican released the document "Priestly Ordination" in which it forbids the ordination of women into the clerical hierarchy of the Catholic Church, proclaiming that the Church has no "authority" to ordain women, and binds it, saying that it must be "definitively be held by all the Church's faithful". It refers to In persona Christi - that the priests during holy orders is the representative of Christ. However, the Church explicitly notes that this does not denigrate the dignity of women referring back to Virgin Mary and the importance of other biblical female figures. This is a long-standing dispute spanning hundreds of years, and has sparked a lot of questioning. One key figure is Rosemary R. Ruether - an American theologian - who raises problems on the patriarchal hierarchy of the governance, saying that it creates a "myth of divine maleness" and that it projects a false image of masculinity onto God - who is sexless. She also notes that this is a cultural issue, not a theological problem, which can definitely be reshaped and reformed. She has even came out to say that the presence of a "male genitalia" is the criterion for ordaining clergy. Other feminist arguments involve citing the Pauline letters, and touching back to the ontological equality and innate dignity God has given to the people, and attacking the fact that this patriarchal governance is to preserve the power and authority of male figures.

Newman proposed seven tests or "notes" for doctrines that develop and change over time, one of them being "Logical Sequence". It asks the question "does the change in doctrine follow a logical sequence from historical events?". Applying this test to this change, we see that (1) God made humankind in His image, (2) all human beings are equal in the eyes of God, therefore (3) all Christians should be given a fair say, so (4) all sexes should also be included in clergy. In this point of view, maintain a male-only hierarchy does not honour the values of DEI.This issue isn't about whether women are included in the church - it is whether they have the same power and authority as men do in guiding the church. Leonardo Boff, a Brazilian theologian, criticizes not only this patriarchal governance, but the whole hierarchical system as a whole. In his book "Ecclesiogenesis", he talks about a Church born "from below". He notes the importance of laity participation in guiding the affairs of the church and the dangers of clericalism. He argues that the "Church is the sacrament of the Holy Spirit", and that clergy should work "with" the laity in the Church. He does not enjoy the idea of a "Pyramid"-like hierarchy, where clerical power is preserved at the summit, while the teachings flow downwards toward the laity, to the base of the pyramid. Boff, in his book "Church, Charism, and Power" also talks about Charism - the gift of the Holy Spirit - and there is no christian who is non-charismatic. Through this individual Charism, one should be able to make an informed decision in the church.

However, a conservative and a traditionalist Catholic view is argued through the lens of apostolic succession and tradition. Without a functional hierarchy, there will be less Catholic-educated clerical power, and prevalent will be one dominated by the common laity, while also losing apostolic relevance to Jesus Christ. A "functional" religious tradition is maintained with relevance to its origin, not relevance to ever-changing current societal affairs and secular feminist movements.

This mechanism of a hierarchy not only enforces guidelines and rules to people who do not have power to control them, but also determines how sacred scripture is "officially" read and interpreted. Lessons and teachings from scripture is interpreted by informed clergy which flows downwards from the summit of Boff's pyramid.

Pope Francis aims to resolve this hierarchal issue with Synod on Synodality - a synod discussing the hierarchal dominance of the Church and clericalism. Francis says that clericalism is the "ugliest mess". The synod's core motif is co-responsibility, where laity is encouraged to participate with clergy to guide the course of the church. This aims to mediate the clericalism accusations, reinforcing the idea that the clergy should serve the laity. In contrast to this hierarchal structure, the Anglican congregational denominations of Christianity do not have a top-bottom structure (flows down from the summit of the "pyramid") but instead a bottom-up structure. The local parishes, diocese and provinces have their own set of rules and interpretations, which make up and inspire the upper congregational synod. But these lower-level provinces still hold communion with the Archbishop of Canterbury - who, in 2025, was elected a woman for the first time to establish a sense of DEI in the Anglican communities. These contrasting examples show the resistance to change of the Catholic Church and its stance for tradition - one may question if changing for the sake of changing is really necessary. This issue on equity, is also an issue on diversity. How equitable is the religious tradition, but at the same time how diverse can the religious tradition get in terms of its power governance?

Since DEI is a relatively new societal issue, we can say that, through Newman's idea, the ongoing dispute between the Church and the societal norms is due to that "imperfect realisation" of the acorn - the core theological idea. The objective moral core God has laid out will take time for humans to reach through the organic development of doctrine. With the hundreds of years of history of the Catholic Church, adapting to new sociological movements is not always necessarily quick. So, then, the Catholic Church can be seen as half-equitable (patriarchal) when it comes to clerical power which does not honour the contemporary values of DEI. In this light, we can interpret the meaning of "equity" as "whether the laity and clergy whom both hold ontological equality can participate in important matters of the church".

The stance of the Catholic church on diversity and equity have varied with the interpretation of "inclusion", which can be explained by Paul Ricoeur's theory of "hermeneutics of suspicion". In 2024, the Vatican published "Fiducia Supplicans", in which it allows priests and bishops to bless, with prudence, non-liturgical "irregular relationships" (This includes the LGBTQ+ couples). However, at the same time it notes that there is no core doctrinal change (marriage is sacred and is only between a man and a woman), but is instead a change for better pastoral care. But this change was not without consequences. The change sparked a lot of backlash, including a meeting for the entirety of the African churches - which has autonomously decided that no African church will bless this irregular relationship. Why would the Vatican change the long-standing stance towards LGBTQ+ communities? This move can be explained through the "hermeneutics of suspicion" - in which it states that texts should be read and interpreted with suspicion, which assumes in the text lies cultural and contextual bias and should not be taken literally. However, Ricoeur also notes it must be interpreted with "restoration" - assuming the text can reveal wisdom, insight, and hope through hermeneutics. This he calls the "Second Naivete" - the hidden meaning only found with hermeneutics of suspicion and restoration. Hermeneutics changes with time, and therefore the resulting interpretation changes with time. The change made by the Catholic Church regarding irregular relationships is a clear example of this hermeneutics. Ricoeur also asks "who benefits from this interpretation?" In this case, it is the marginalised but important figures of humanity that benefits. Historically there was no large issues with the LGBTQ+ community on homosexual relationships and the Church (primarily due to these groups staying under the radar as to not face persecution). However, as the societal norms become more progressive, these issues are brought into light and through hermeneutics must be resolved by the Church. We must remember that scripture, which does not change, is God's way of staying relevant to the ever-changing societies - to be interpreted with hermeneutical lenses of suspicion and restoration.

We may also apply two of the seven tests of Newman to this change to strengthen its evaluation. The two new tests are "Preservation of Type" - which states that the change should be a change of outward expression, not the core doctrine, and "Anticipation of Future" - which asks, have we seen the seed of this change before in history? First, Fiducia supplicans preserves the core doctrine of the Church (marriage is between a man and a woman). Next, we have seen a seed of this change before in history. Jesus Christ, believed to be the Son of God, exemplified best the values of compassion and mercy - two founding values of Fiducia Supplicans - by treating sinners and criminals with love and pastoral care. So it passes the Anticipation of Future test - we have seen this before. Therefore, this is a step closer towards the marginalised groups of people by the Catholic Church.

Through this and other efforts of inclusion by the church (including the change in stance towards slaves), the Catholic Church explores what inclusion means in a DEI context. It approaches the definition of "the participation of the marginalised" - promoting the importance of inclusion in all aspects of Catholic life.

One change that could be introduced to the Catholic Church may be related to Base Ecclesial Groups - small (originally Catholic, but now also includes all Christians) groups of laypeople reading scripture and interpreting it in the light of their experiences. This movement is prominent in impoverished and marginalised areas, especially Latin America. Leonardo Boff was a strong advocate for these Base Ecclesial Groups - saying that the interpretation of texts and the influences of its teachings are not systemically introduced by elected hierarchal powers, instead it is carried out by those on the margins, those without immense clerical authority. Here, hermeneutics has a great weighting, as the interpretation of texts by those directly affected by their socioeconomic status can be immensely different from an interpretation by a highly informed and well-educated priest. If the society moves toward a stronger sense of DEI, and the Church stays faithful to its traditional roots, then who knows? More laypeople may branch off from Catholicism into these Base Ecclesial Groups - potentially starting a Church with like-minded people. This passes the "Anticipation of Future" test by Newman - we have seen this kind of reformation before starting in the 1500s, such as the German reformations.

The three definitions of DEI we have explored: "unity in plurality", "ontological equality and their say on important matters", and "the participation of the marginalised" are all interconnected and interdependent. Plurality creates unity, which envisions important matters to be governed with ontological equality, which involves the participation of all people, including the marginalised. The Catholic Church have explored these issues, have grown and changed its traditions, but were not without consequences; however, with all change and reformation comes backlash. You cannot please everybody. And just like today, the Catholic Church will continuously live on the edge between tradition and commitment, and relevance and reformations. The challenge lies between the gaps - how can it stay faithful to its core teachings and history, while still adapting to the ever-growing human development of the mind and sociological behaviours? The Church will continue to navigate its religious traditions to honour the values of DEI - receiving backlash, retrieving its teachings, so it can converge to that sum - that objective moral line where humans will continuously fall short, but exert an organic growth to climb back up to it.

I believe God will guide the Church and informs all mankind, such that it honours diversity, inclusion and equity to promote the life of Christ.
